  Helpers: unconstrained=UnconstrainedStep (token-by-token); delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=AdaptiveConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix
  Rationale: The last verified strategy was close on accuracy but failed the required
`Contains << >>` threshold because it almost never actually opened a
constrained span: the trace shows only 11 opens over 100 examples. The main
issue was architectural, not arithmetic decoding quality. It spent many
unconstrained steps repeatedly "considering" `<<` by boosting it, then fell
back to ordinary unconstrained sampling, so the model often never committed
to the delimiter before the answer ended.
This revision fixes that by making a single arithmetic span mandatory and
early, while still leaving a short natural-language prelude. Concretely:
(1) before any span has been opened, we allow only a tiny warmup of a few
unconstrained tokens to produce step-by-step lead-in text;
(2) once the warmup is over, if `<<` is available we open the constrained
span directly with `OpenConstrainedSpan` instead of merely boosting it;
(3) inside the span, we use parser-valid constrained decoding every step and
close immediately once the parser says the arithmetic fragment is complete;
(4) after closing the first span, we strongly prefer a short finish by
boosting EOS and penalizing reopening `<<`.
This directly addresses the measured failures. Mandatory direct opening
removes the missing-segment problem. Immediate parser-checked closure keeps
syntax high. A very short prelude preserves "step by step" wording better
than opening at token 0, while avoiding the previous trace pattern of dozens
of unconstrained tokens with no span. Using AdaptiveConstrainedStep inside
the span also helps malformed constrained content by relying on parser-valid
next-token masking rather than free-form arithmetic prose.

